  "text": "Question ten. What is the difference between additive, semi-additive, and non-additive measures? An amount is additive: it sums across every dimension. A balance is semi-additive. Summed across accounts on one day, it's right: sixteen hundred dollars at the end of March. Summed across months, it's nonsense: the quarter's three month-ends add to forty-eight hundred, money that never existed. Over time, take the last value in each period, or the average. Non-additive measures, like ratios and distinct counts, don't sum at all. Three users on Monday and three on Tuesday are four people, not six. Store the parts, and compute the ratio in the query.",
